];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Are ravens just big crows?', a: 'No. Ravens (Corvus corax and related species) and crows (Corvus corone, C. brachyrhynchos, etc.) are distinct species within the genus Corvus. They are related — all are corvids — but they are not the same animal at different sizes. Ravens differ from crows in tail shape, bill size, throat hackles, flight behaviour, calls, habitat, and behaviour.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens in the crow family?', a: 'Yes. Ravens belong to the family Corvidae (the crow family), genus Corvus. All crows, ravens, jackdaws, rooks, and choughs are corvids. The common name "crow" is imprecise — it is used for several distinct Corvus species, while "raven" refers specifically to Common Raven (Corvus corax) and related large Corvus species.' },
  { q: 'What is the difference between a raven and a crow?', a: 'Key differences: tail shape (wedge on raven, fan on crow), size (raven roughly twice the weight of Carrion Crow), bill (raven bill heavier with shaggy throat hackles), voice (raven croaks, crow caws), and behaviour (ravens soar and play aerially; crows mostly flap). See the full comparison at the Raven vs Crow page.' },
];
---

